---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import MandalaTool from "../tools/MandalaTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="mandala">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        Radial patterns at any size and any symmetry, drawn the way a mandala actually is:
        <strong class="text-slate-300">line work</strong>, with a smaller echo of every motif inside itself. Engrave
        them into a disc, or cut them clean through for a suncatcher — in which case the number that matters is not
        how it looks but how much material is left between the motifs, and that is measured for you in millimetres.
    </Fragment>

    <MandalaTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </MandalaTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Cut, the pattern is the only structure</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Engraved, a mandala cannot go wrong: it is a burn on a disc. Cut through, it is
                    <em>holes</em> in a disc, and holes that meet each other separate the disc into pieces. A mandala
                    that will fall into forty petals on the bed looks exactly like one that will not — same drawing,
                    same colours, and no warning until you lift it.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    So two numbers are worked out from the parameters rather than left to be judged by eye: the
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">web</strong> between one motif and the next at its widest point, and
                    the material <strong class="text-slate-300">between rings</strong>. Those two are the whole
                    structure; everything else is a hole. Under about 2 mm a cut disc is fragile in anything but
                    plywood, and under 1 mm it is not a disc any more.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Line work, and an echo</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Two things separate a mandala from a pattern of blobs, and neither is the shape of the motif.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    The first is that a mandala is <strong class="text-slate-300">outlined</strong>. Every one ever
                    drawn is line work — the shapes are drawn as outlines and only sometimes filled in afterwards — and
                    solid areas are the single thing that makes a generated one look generated. It is also a fraction
                    of the burn. The second is the <strong class="text-slate-300">nested echo</strong>: a smaller copy
                    of each motif inside itself, which is what turns a shape into a motif. Both are on by default and
                    both can be turned off; the echo is engraved only, because a smaller hole inside a hole is a ring
                    of material that falls out on its own.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Eight motifs, and why these eight</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    They are not arbitrary. Every guide to drawing a mandala reaches for the same handful — teardrops,
                    pointed petals, dots, triangles, arcs, diamonds — because those are the shapes that survive being
                    repeated thirty times without turning to mush.
                </p>
                <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-4 text-sm leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Lotus petals</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Drawn out to a sharp point at each end — the shape most people picture when they hear the word.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Petals</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A lens: widest in the middle, pointed at both ends.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Drops</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Narrow at the hub and round at the rim, like a flame. Reads as motion round the circle.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Diamonds</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Straight sides to a point either side — the one angular motif, and what makes a ring read as geometry rather than flowers.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Darts</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Triangles standing on the hub and widening to the rim. One ring of them on a big hub is a sunburst.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Spokes</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Nearly parallel sides with rounded ends. The most open of the set and the strongest when cut — this is the one for a suncatcher.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Scallops</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">Fat almost all the way out, so what is left between them is a thin rib. Handsome engraved and the first to break cut.</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Dots</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">A ring of small circles instead of a band of shapes. Structural rather than decorative: without one, a run of bands reads as concentric fences.</dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
            </div>

            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Every ring is turned half a slot</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Rings whose motifs sat at the same angles would read as spokes running all the way from the hub
                        to the rim, which is a wheel rather than a mandala. Every other ring is offset by half its
                        slot, so the pattern reads as a weave — and it puts material where the neighbouring ring has a
                        hole, which is also why a cut one holds.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Few controls, on purpose</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        There is no slider per ring. That would be more powerful and would produce worse mandalas: what
                        makes one look deliberate is that everything in it follows from the same two or three
                        decisions. Symmetry, rings and the web move the whole drawing at once, and the
                        <em>mixed</em> motif picks a different shape per ring from a seed you can go back to.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                Every dimension is a fraction of the diameter, so the pattern is the same shape at 40 mm and at 400 —
                but the web is not, because it is a fraction of a circle that got smaller. A design that survives being
                cut at 200 mm can fall apart at 80.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
